    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "As the threshold increases, the classifier becomes more conservative in predicting the positive class. This often reduces false positives and increases precision, but it may also increase false negatives and reduce recall.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Selecting a Threshold on Validation Data\n",
        "\n",
        "The threshold should not be selected using the final test set. A common workflow is:\n",
        "\n",
        "1. split the available training data into a model-training set and a validation set;\n",
        "2. fit the logistic regression model on the model-training set;\n",
        "3. evaluate candidate thresholds on the validation set;\n",
        "4. refit the model on the full training set if desired;\n",
        "5. evaluate once on the test set.\n",
        "\n",
        "The following example chooses the threshold that maximizes validation F1 score."
      ],
      "id": "d191be27"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X_model, X_valid, y_model, y_valid = train_test_split(\n",
        "    X_train,\n",
        "    y_train,\n",
        "    test_size=0.25,\n",
        "    stratify=y_train,\n",
        "    random_state=11,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "validation_model = make_pipeline(\n",
        "    StandardScaler(),\n",
        "    LogisticRegression(penalty=None, max_iter=5000),\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "validation_model.fit(X_model, y_model)\n",
        "valid_prob = validation_model.predict_proba(X_valid)[:, 1]\n",
        "\n",
        "candidate_thresholds = np.linspace(0.05, 0.95, 91)\n",
        "\n",
        "validation_results = pd.DataFrame(\n",
        "    [\n",
        "        classification_summary(y_valid, valid_prob, threshold=t)\n",
        "        for t in candidate_thresholds\n",
        "    ]\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "best_f1_row = validation_results.loc[\n",
        "    validation_results[\"f1\"].idxmax()\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "best_f1_row"
      ],
      "id": "83ac6fc9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The selected threshold can then be applied to the test probabilities from the original fitted model."
      ],
      "id": "1b70383a"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "best_threshold = best_f1_row[\"threshold\"]\n",
        "\n",
        "classification_summary(\n",
        "    y_test,\n",
        "    test_prob,\n",
        "    threshold=best_threshold,\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "d9dc297c",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The same idea can be used for other goals. For example, we may want the largest recall among thresholds whose precision is at least $0.90$."
      ],
      "id": "a8e03024"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "valid_candidates = validation_results[\n",
        "    validation_results[\"precision\"] >= 0.90\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "best_recall_row = valid_candidates.loc[\n",
        "    valid_candidates[\"recall\"].idxmax()\n",
        "]\n",
        "\n",
        "best_recall_row"
      ],
      "id": "6fa406e5",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "classification_summary(\n",
        "    y_test,\n",
        "    test_prob,\n",
        "    threshold=best_recall_row[\"threshold\"],\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "8078c0f8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Fixed and Tuned Thresholds in `sklearn`\n",
        "\n",
        "Manual thresholding is often the clearest method. `sklearn` also provides wrappers that make the threshold part of the estimator workflow.\n",
        "\n",
        "If the threshold is fixed in advance, use `FixedThresholdClassifier`."
      ],
      "id": "8d788d34"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import FixedThresholdClassifier\n",
        "\n",
        "fixed_threshold_model = FixedThresholdClassifier(\n",
        "    make_pipeline(\n",
        "        StandardScaler(),\n",
        "        LogisticRegression(penalty=None, max_iter=5000),\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    threshold=0.3,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "fixed_threshold_model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "fixed_pred = fixed_threshold_model.predict(X_test)\n",
        "\n",
        "confusion_matrix(y_test, fixed_pred)"
      ],
      "id": "b2ce27ea",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "If the threshold should be selected from data, use `TunedThresholdClassifierCV`. This performs cross-validation to choose a threshold for a specified scoring criterion."
      ],
      "id": "510ff1d0"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import TunedThresholdClassifierCV\n",
        "\n",
        "tuned_threshold_model = TunedThresholdClassifierCV(\n",
        "    estimator=make_pipeline(\n",
        "        StandardScaler(),\n",
        "        LogisticRegression(penalty=None, max_iter=5000),\n",
        "    ),\n",
        "    scoring=\"recall\",\n",
        "    cv=5,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "tuned_threshold_model.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "tuned_pred = tuned_threshold_model.predict(X_test)\n",
        "\n",
        "{\n",
        "    \"accuracy\": accuracy_score(y_test, tuned_pred),\n",
        "    \"precision\": precision_score(y_test, tuned_pred),\n",
        "    \"recall\": recall_score(y_test, tuned_pred),\n",
        "    \"f1\": f1_score(y_test, tuned_pred),\n",
        "}"
      ],
      "id": "08fe0ac7",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-caution}\n",
        "## Tune thresholds on training data only\n",
        "\n",
        "The threshold should be chosen using training data or validation data, not the final test set. The test set should be used only for the final evaluation.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Choosing a Threshold from Costs\n",
        "\n",
        "In a medical screening setting, missing a malignant tumor may be more costly than sending a benign case for additional testing. Suppose\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C_{FN}=5,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "C_{FP}=1.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The cost-based threshold is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "c^*\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{C_{FP}}{C_{FP}+C_{FN}}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{1}{1+5}\n",
        "\\approx\n",
        "0.167.\n",
        "$$"
      ],
      "id": "45e349e8"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "cost_fp = 1\n",
        "cost_fn = 5\n",
        "\n",
        "cost_threshold = cost_fp / (cost_fp + cost_fn)\n",
        "\n",
        "classification_summary(\n",
        "    y_test,\n",
        "    test_prob,\n",
        "    threshold=cost_threshold,\n",
        ")"
      ],
      "id": "a499e613",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "This lower threshold prioritizes recall. It predicts malignant more aggressively because false negatives are assumed to be more costly.\n",
        "\n",
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "The cost-based threshold formula assumes that the fitted probabilities are reasonably calibrated.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## ROC Curve and AUC\n",
        "\n",
        "ROC analysis evaluates ranking quality across thresholds. It does not depend on a single fixed threshold.\n",
        "\n",
        "For a threshold $c$, define\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "TPR(c)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\Pr(\\hat p(X)>c\\mid Y=1)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{TP(c)}{TP(c)+FN(c)}\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "FPR(c)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\Pr(\\hat p(X)>c\\mid Y=0)\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac{FP(c)}{FP(c)+TN(c)}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The ROC curve plots $TPR(c)$ against $FPR(c)$ as the threshold varies over the range of possible scores."
      ],
      "id": "5d1f8e36"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.metrics import roc_curve, roc_auc_score\n",
        "\n",
        "fpr, tpr, roc_thresholds = roc_curve(y_test, test_prob)\n",
        "auc = roc_auc_score(y_test, test_prob)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.plot(fpr, tpr, label=f\"Logistic regression AUC = {auc:.3f}\")\n",
        "plt.plot([0, 1], [0, 1], \"k--\", label=\"random guessing\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"false positive rate\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"true positive rate\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"ROC Curve\")\n",
        "plt.legend()"
      ],
      "id": "af673630",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "AUC is the area under the ROC curve. It can be interpreted as\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "AUC\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\Pr\\left(s(X^+)>s(X^-)\\right),\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "up to small corrections for ties, where $X^+$ is a randomly selected positive observation, $X^-$ is a randomly selected negative observation, and $s(\\cdot)$ is the model score.\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus AUC evaluates ranking quality. It does not evaluate whether the predicted probabilities are numerically calibrated.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Precision-Recall Curve\n",
        "\n",
        "Precision-recall curves are often useful when the positive class is rare or when performance on the positive class is the main concern."
      ],
      "id": "13da4588"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.metrics import precision_recall_curve, average_precision_score\n",
        "\n",
        "precision, recall, pr_thresholds = precision_recall_curve(y_test, test_prob)\n",
        "avg_precision = average_precision_score(y_test, test_prob)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.plot(recall, precision, label=f\"AP = {avg_precision:.3f}\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"recall\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"precision\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Precision-Recall Curve\")\n",
        "plt.legend()"
      ],
      "id": "f49d9231",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Average precision summarizes the precision-recall curve. Compared with ROC AUC, it is often more sensitive to false positives when the positive class is rare.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Probability Quality\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression directly estimates probabilities, so probability quality is also important.\n",
        "\n",
        "Two common proper scoring rules are Brier score and log loss:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{Brier}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "(y_i-\\hat p_i)^2,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\text{LogLoss}\n",
        "=\n",
        "-\\frac1n\n",
        "\\sum_{i=1}^n\n",
        "\\left[\n",
        "y_i\\log(\\hat p_i)\n",
        "+\n",
        "(1-y_i)\\log(1-\\hat p_i)\n",
        "\\right].\n",
        "$$"
      ],
      "id": "a743e435"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.metrics import brier_score_loss, log_loss\n",
        "\n",
        "probability_scores = {\n",
        "    \"brier_score\": brier_score_loss(y_test, test_prob),\n",
        "    \"log_loss\": log_loss(y_test, test_prob),\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "probability_scores"
      ],
      "id": "ae27da23",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "Calibration curves diagnose whether predicted probabilities are numerically meaningful."
      ],
      "id": "9b6a178d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.calibration import CalibrationDisplay\n",
        "\n",
        "CalibrationDisplay.from_predictions(\n",
        "    y_test,\n",
        "    test_prob,\n",
        "    n_bins=10,\n",
        "    name=\"Logistic regression\",\n",
        ")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Calibration Curve\")"
      ],
      "id": "c00252ec",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "If a model predicts probability near $0.8$, then a well-calibrated model should have an observed positive frequency near $80\\%$ among observations with similar predicted probabilities.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Decision Boundary\n",
        "\n",
        "With two predictors, we can visualize the fitted probability surface and the decision boundary."
      ],
      "id": "8243e86b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "X2 = cancer.data[[\"mean radius\", \"mean texture\"]]\n",
        "y2 = 1 - cancer.target\n",
        "\n",
        "X2_train, X2_test, y2_train, y2_test = train_test_split(\n",
        "    X2,\n",
        "    y2,\n",
        "    test_size=0.3,\n",
        "    stratify=y2,\n",
        "    random_state=2,\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "boundary_model = make_pipeline(\n",
        "    StandardScaler(),\n",
        "    LogisticRegression(penalty=None, max_iter=5000),\n",
        ")\n",
        "boundary_model.fit(X2_train, y2_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "x0_min, x0_max = X2.iloc[:, 0].min() - 1, X2.iloc[:, 0].max() + 1\n",
        "x1_min, x1_max = X2.iloc[:, 1].min() - 1, X2.iloc[:, 1].max() + 1\n",
        "\n",
        "xx0, xx1 = np.meshgrid(\n",
        "    np.linspace(x0_min, x0_max, 300),\n",
        "    np.linspace(x1_min, x1_max, 300),\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "grid_points = pd.DataFrame(\n",
        "    {\n",
        "        \"mean radius\": xx0.ravel(),\n",
        "        \"mean texture\": xx1.ravel(),\n",
        "    }\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "zz = boundary_model.predict_proba(grid_points)[:, 1].reshape(xx0.shape)\n",
        "\n",
        "plt.figure(figsize=(7, 5))\n",
        "plt.contourf(xx0, xx1, zz, levels=20, alpha=0.35)\n",
        "plt.contour(xx0, xx1, zz, levels=[0.5], colors=\"black\", linewidths=2)\n",
        "plt.scatter(X2_test.iloc[:, 0], X2_test.iloc[:, 1], c=y2_test, edgecolor=\"k\")\n",
        "plt.xlabel(\"mean radius\")\n",
        "plt.ylabel(\"mean texture\")\n",
        "plt.title(\"Logistic Regression Probability Surface\")\n",
        "plt.show()"
      ],
      "id": "0cea224a",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The $0.5$ decision boundary is linear in the original predictors because the log-odds are linear. Other thresholds produce parallel boundaries in the two-predictor unpenalized logistic regression model.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Penalized Logistic Regression\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression can be regularized in the same spirit as ridge regression and lasso.\n",
        "\n",
        "The unpenalized logistic regression estimator solves\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat\\beta\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\arg\\min_\\beta\n",
        "\\left\\{\n",
        "-\\ell(\\beta)\n",
        "\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Ridge logistic regression solves\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat\\beta_{\\ridge}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\arg\\min_\\beta\n",
        "\\left\\{\n",
        "-\\ell(\\beta)\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\lambda\\sum_{j=1}^p\\beta_j^2\n",
        "\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Lasso logistic regression solves\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\hat\\beta_{\\lasso}\n",
        "=\n",
        "\\arg\\min_\\beta\n",
        "\\left\\{\n",
        "-\\ell(\\beta)\n",
        "+\n",
        "\\lambda\\sum_{j=1}^p|\\beta_j|\n",
        "\\right\\}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The intercept is usually not penalized. Predictors should usually be standardized before penalized logistic regression because the penalty depends on coefficient scale.\n",
        "\n",
        "In `sklearn`, the parameter `C` controls inverse regularization strength:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C=\\frac{1}{\\lambda}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Smaller `C` means stronger regularization."
      ],
      "id": "d56f0162"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV\n",
        "\n",
        "penalized_pipe = make_pipeline(\n",
        "    StandardScaler(),\n",
        "    LogisticRegression(\n",
        "        penalty=\"l1\",\n",
        "        solver=\"liblinear\",\n",
        "        max_iter=5000,\n",
        "    ),\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "param_grid = {\n",
        "    \"logisticregression__C\": np.logspace(-3, 2, 20),\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "grid = GridSearchCV(\n",
        "    penalized_pipe,\n",
        "    param_grid=param_grid,\n",
        "    cv=5,\n",
        "    scoring=\"roc_auc\",\n",
        ")\n",
        "\n",
        "grid.fit(X_train, y_train)\n",
        "\n",
        "grid.best_params_"
      ],
      "id": "389844d9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The selected model is available as:"
      ],
      "id": "9d2fc567"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "grid.best_estimator_"
      ],
      "id": "afdf87ab",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "::: {.callout-note}\n",
        "## Regularization and model tuning\n",
        "\n",
        "Regularization changes the fitted probabilities by changing the estimated coefficients. Threshold tuning changes only the final decision rule.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Summary\n",
        "\n",
        "Logistic regression:\n",
        "\n",
        "- models binary outcomes through class probabilities;\n",
        "- uses the logit link to make log-odds linear in the predictors;\n",
        "- estimates coefficients by maximum likelihood;\n",
        "- interprets coefficients through odds ratios;\n",
        "- produces both probabilities and class predictions;\n",
        "- requires a threshold for hard classification;\n",
        "- can be evaluated by threshold-based metrics, ROC AUC, precision-recall curves, and probability-quality metrics;\n",
        "- is often combined with regularization in predictive workflows.\n",
        "\n",
        "Use `sklearn` when prediction, pipelines, cross-validation, threshold tuning, and model selection are the main goal."
      ],
      "id": "f1cf81ad"
    }
